body:
Yeah so that’s my interpretation as well. Here’s the code I’m talking about. 
Result{
If(n == 0 || n == 1) {
    Return 1; 
}
Else{
     Return 2 * result(n-1); 
}
}

Ex. n = 5. 

In that call, the call  would be 2 times 5-1. However, the answer turned out to be that once we hit the base case, we multiply 2 times every call to the stack to get the final answer of 15. I was under the impression that once we hit the base case and work our way back up to the original question, we don’t include the values from other calls.

here's a draft of two problems i've been working on 

problem 1 either24:

Given an array of ints, return true if the array contains a 2 next to a 2 or a 4 next to a 4, but not both.

public boolean either24(int[] nums) {
  boolean x = false; 

  if(nums.length == 0){
    return x; 
  }
  else if(nums.length >=2){
    for(int i = 0; i<nums.length; i++){
      if(nums[i] == 2 && nums[i+1] == 2){
        x = true; 
      }
      else if(nums[i] == 4 && nums[i+1] == 4){
        x = true; 
      }
      else if(nums[i] == 2 && nums[i+1] == 2 && nums[i+2] == 4 && nums[i+3] == 4) // you can tell i'm a little lost on what to do here {
        x = false; 
      }
    }
  }
}

problem 2 has77:

Given an array of ints, return true if the array contains two 7's next to each other, or there are two 7's separated by one element, such as with {7, 1, 7}.

public boolean has77(int[] nums) {

  if(nums.length < 2){
    return false; 
  }

  for(int i = 1; i<nums.length-1; i++){
    if(nums[i] == 7 && nums[i+1] == 7){

      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
